**Cause:** Floating-point numbers in JS use IEEE 754 double precision, which can't represent most decimal fractions exactly. `0.1 + 0.2 + 0.3` accumulates rounding error, producing `0.6000000000000001` instead of `0.6`. Comparing with `!==` demands exact equality, which binary floats can't guarantee for decimal arithmetic.

**Fix:** Don't compare floats for exact equality — use an epsilon-based comparison, or better, work in integer cents to avoid floating-point math entirely.

Epsilon approach:
```javascript
const EPSILON = 1e-9;
if (Math.abs(total - 0.6) > EPSILON) {
  throw new Error(`cart total mismatch: ${total}`);
}
```

Integer-cents approach (more robust for money):
```javascript
const pricesInCents = [10, 20, 30];
let totalCents = 0;
for (const p of pricesInCents) {
  totalCents += p;
}
if (totalCents !== 60) {
  throw new Error(`cart total mismatch: ${totalCents}`);
}
```

For real cart/money logic, prefer integer cents (or a decimal library like `decimal.js`) over floats altogether.

