## Cause

JavaScript numbers use the IEEE 754 double-precision format. This format can't represent most decimal fractions exactly, so `0.1 + 0.2 + 0.3` produces `0.6000000000000001` instead of `0.6`. The strict equality check `total !== 0.6` then fails, even though the sum is correct for practical purposes.

## Fix

Don't compare floating-point sums with `!==`. Use one of these approaches instead.

**Option 1: Compare with a small tolerance (epsilon)**

```javascript
const EPSILON = 1e-9;
if (Math.abs(total - 0.6) > EPSILON) {
  throw new Error(`cart total mismatch: ${total}`);
}
```

**Option 2: Work in integer cents**

Convert prices to integer cents before summing, so the arithmetic stays exact.

```javascript
const pricesInCents = [10, 20, 30];
let totalInCents = 0;
for (const p of pricesInCents) {
  totalInCents += p;
}
if (totalInCents !== 60) {
  throw new Error(`cart total mismatch: ${totalInCents / 100}`);
}
```

For currency calculations, the integer-cents approach is the more robust choice, because it avoids floating-point rounding errors throughout the calculation, not just at the final comparison.

## Cause

Computers store decimal numbers like `0.1` and `0.2` in binary floating-point format. Binary can't represent most decimal fractions exactly, so the values get tiny rounding errors. When you add them up, the errors combine, and you get `0.6000000000000001` instead of `0.6`. The `!==` check then fails, even though the total is correct for all practical purposes.

## Fix

Don't compare floating-point numbers with `===` or `!==`. Instead, check whether the difference between the two values is smaller than a tiny tolerance (called an "epsilon"):

```javascript
const prices = [0.1, 0.2, 0.3];
let total = 0;
for (const p of prices) {
  total += p;
}

const EPSILON = 1e-9;
if (Math.abs(total - 0.6) > EPSILON) {
  throw new Error(`cart total mismatch: ${total}`);
}
```

## Alternative fix for money

If you're working with prices, a more reliable approach is to do all math in integer cents instead of decimal dollars. Integers don't have this rounding problem:

```javascript
const pricesInCents = [10, 20, 30]; // $0.10, $0.20, $0.30
const totalCents = pricesInCents.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p, 0);

if (totalCents !== 60) {
  throw new Error(`cart total mismatch: ${totalCents}`);
}
```

Convert to dollars (`totalCents / 100`) only when you display the value to the user.

